{
  "slug": "artist:miles-davis",
  "name": "Miles Davis",
  "editorial_article": "Miles Dewey Davis III (1926–1991) was an American trumpeter, bandleader, and composer who stands as one of the most transformative figures in the history of 20th-century music. Over a career spanning nearly five decades, Davis was not merely a participant in jazz evolution; he was its primary architect, repeatedly steering the music into uncharted territories. Beginning his professional journey in the 1940s within Charlie Parker's bebop quintet, Davis quickly moved to define the 'cool jazz' aesthetic with his Birth of the Cool sessions. Throughout the 1950s and 60s, he pioneered hard bop, modal jazz, and post-bop, assembling some of the most legendary lineups in jazz history, including the sextet that recorded the monumental Kind of Blue (1959).\n\nDavis's restlessness as an artist eventually led him to dismantle the traditional structures of jazz entirely. In the late 1960s and early 1970s, he embraced electric instrumentation, rock rhythms, and funk, sparking the jazz fusion movement with seminal works like Bitches Brew (1970). This period challenged and polarized the jazz establishment but solidified his legacy as a restless innovator. After a hiatus in the late 1970s due to health issues, Davis returned in the 1980s, engaging with contemporary pop and electronic sounds before his death in 1991. His influence is inescapable, resonating far beyond jazz into the foundations of rock, funk, and hip-hop. He remains the definitive archetype of the cool, intellectual, and uncompromising artist. His contributions redefined the possibilities of improvisation and orchestration, ensuring his place as an eternal icon of artistic evolution. Through his constant reinvention, he kept the genre vital for successive generations.",
